> trig sin^2 x
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of sin^2 x
  power reduction: sin^2 u = (1 - cos 2u)/2, cos^2 u = (1 + cos 2u)/2 (higher powers through e^(iu) = cos u + i sin u)
    = (1 - cos(2x))/2
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 1e-16)
  exponential (Euler): sin u = (e^(iu) - e^(-iu))/(2i)
    = ((exp(i*x) - exp(-i*x))/(2*i))^2
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points
  simplest: sin^2 x  (as given)
> trig sin(x) cos(x)
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of sin(x) cos(x)
  product to sum: sin a cos b = (sin(a + b) + sin(a - b))/2, cos a cos b = (cos(a - b) + cos(a + b))/2, sin a sin b = (cos(a - b) - cos(a + b))/2
    = sin(2x)/2
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 6e-17)
  exponential (Euler): sin u = (e^(iu) - e^(-iu))/(2i), cos u = (e^(iu) + e^(-iu))/2
    = (exp(i*x) - exp(-i*x))*(exp(i*x) + exp(-i*x))/(4*i)
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points
  simplest: sin(2x)/2  (product to sum)
