  w0 = 1                      = 1  (principal)
  w1 = 1/2 + i*sqrt(3)/2      = e^(i*pi/3)
  w2 = -1/2 + i*sqrt(3)/2     = e^(i*2pi/3)
  w3 = -1                     = e^(i*pi)
  w4 = -1/2 - i*sqrt(3)/2     = e^(-i*2pi/3)
  w5 = 1/2 - i*sqrt(3)/2      = e^(-i*pi/3)
  check: each w^6, parsed back as printed, equals 1 numerically
> what is the principal argument of -1
  ucas: arg -1
arg(-1) = pi
  the principal argument, -pi < arg <= pi
  check: equal numerically (3.141592654, complex arithmetic)
> use the double angle formula on cos 2x
  ucas: trig cos 2x double
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of cos 2x
  compound and multiple angles: sin(a + b) = sin a cos b + cos a sin b, cos(a + b) = cos a cos b - sin a sin b
    = cos(x)^2 - sin(x)^2
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  compound angles, in sin: cos^2 u = 1 - sin^2 u
    = 1 - 2sin(x)^2
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  compound angles, in cos: sin^2 u = 1 - cos^2 u
    = 2cos(x)^2 - 1
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 3e-16)
  simplest: cos 2x  (as given)
> turn sin x cos y into a sum
  ucas: trig sin x cos y product
  note: x, y are taken as real
trig forms of sin x cos y
  product to sum: sin a cos b = (sin(a + b) + sin(a - b))/2, cos a cos b = (cos(a - b) + cos(a + b))/2, sin a sin b = (cos(a - b) - cos(a + b))/2
    = (sin(x + y) + sin(x - y))/2
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 1e-16)
  simplest: sin x cos y  (as given)
> write sin(x + pi/4) using the angle addition formula
  ucas: trig sin(x + pi/4) expand
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of sin(x + pi/4)
  compound and multiple angles: sin(a + b) = sin a cos b + cos a sin b, cos(a + b) = cos a cos b - sin a sin b
    = sqrt(2)*sin(x)/2 + sqrt(2)*cos(x)/2
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  simplest: sin(x + pi/4)  (as given)
> express sin x + cos x as a single sine
  ucas: trig sin x + cos x harmonic
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of sin x + cos x
  amplitude-phase: a sin u + b cos u = R sin(u + phi), R = sqrt(a^2 + b^2), phi = atan2(b, a)
    = sqrt(2)*sin(x + pi/4)
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  amplitude-phase: a sin u + b cos u = R cos(u - psi), psi = atan2(a, b)
    = sqrt(2)*cos(x - pi/4)
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 3e-16)
  simplest: sin x + cos x  (as given)
> write tanh x in terms of exponentials
  ucas: trig tanh x exp
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of tanh x
  exponential (Euler): tanh u = (e^u - e^(-u))/(e^u + e^(-u))
    = (exp(x) - exp(-x))/(exp(x) + exp(-x))
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  simplest: tanh x  (as given)
> rewrite acos x in terms of asin
  ucas: trig acos x inverse
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of acos x
  acos via asin: acos(u) = pi/2 - asin(u)
    = pi/2 - asin(x)
    check: equal at 31 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  acos via asin: acos(u) = asin(sqrt(1 - u^2))
    = asin(sqrt(1 - x^2))
    for 0 <= x <= 1
    check: equal at 18 of 64 sample points where 0 <= x <= 1 (max relative difference 2e-16)
      (outside: differs at 13 of 13 points, so the condition is needed)
  acos via asin: acos(u) = 2asin(sqrt((1 - u)/2))
    = 2asin(sqrt((1 - x)/2))
    check: equal at 31 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  acos via atan: acos(u) = atan(sqrt(1 - u^2)/u)
    = atan(sqrt(1 - x^2)/x)
    for 0 < x <= 1
    check: equal at 18 of 64 sample points where 0 < x <= 1 (max relative difference 2e-16)
      (outside: differs at 13 of 13 points, so the condition is needed)
  acos via atan: acos(u) = pi + atan(sqrt(1 - u^2)/u)
    = atan(sqrt(1 - x^2)/x) + pi
    for -1 <= x < 0
    check: equal at 13 of 64 sample points where -1 <= x < 0 (max relative difference 2e-16)
      (outside: differs at 18 of 18 points, so the condition is needed)
  acos half-angle: acos(u) = 2atan(sqrt((1 - u)/(1 + u)))
    = 2atan(sqrt((1 - x)/(x + 1)))
    for -1 < x <= 1
    check: equal at 31 of 64 sample points where -1 < x <= 1 (max relative difference 2e-16)
  acos as atan2: acos(u) = atan2(sqrt(1 - u^2), u)
    = atan2(sqrt(1 - x^2), x)
    check: equal at 31 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  simplest: acos x  (as given)
> simplify cos(atan x)
  ucas: trig cos(atan x)
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of cos(atan x)
  simplified: sin^2 + cos^2 = 1, cosh^2 - sinh^2 = 1, f(f^-1(u)) = u
    = 1/sqrt(x^2 + 1)
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
  simplest: 1/sqrt(x^2 + 1)  (simplified)
> how do I write (2+i)/(1-i) in standard form
  ucas: complex (2+i)/(1-i)
(2+i)/(1-i) = 1/2 + 3i/2
  Re = 1/2,  Im = 3/2
  check: equal numerically (0.5 + 1.5i, complex arithmetic)
> what is the trigonometric form of 1 + i
  ucas: polar 1 + i
1 + i = sqrt(2)*e^(i*pi/4)
  = sqrt(2)*(cos(pi/4) + i*sin(pi/4))
  r = |z| = sqrt(2),  theta = arg z = pi/4
  theta is the principal argument, -pi < theta <= pi
  check: equal numerically (1 + 1i, complex arithmetic)
  check: equal numerically (1 + 1i, complex arithmetic)
> power reduction formula for cos^4 x
  ucas: trig cos^4 x reduce
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of cos^4 x
  power reduction: sin^2 u = (1 - cos 2u)/2, cos^2 u = (1 + cos 2u)/2 (higher powers through e^(iu) = cos u + i sin u)
    = (4cos(2x) + cos(4x) + 3)/8
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 3e-16)
  simplest: cos^4 x  (as given)
> alternative forms of tan x
  ucas: trig tan x
  note: x is taken as real
trig forms of tan x
  in sin and cos: tan = sin/cos, sec = 1/cos, csc = 1/sin, cot = cos/sin, tanh = sinh/cosh
    = sin(x)/cos(x)
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 4e-16)
  exponential (Euler): tan u = (e^(iu) - e^(-iu))/(i(e^(iu) + e^(-iu)))
    = (exp(i*x) - exp(-i*x))/(i*(exp(i*x) + exp(-i*x)))
    check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 4e-16)
  simplest: tan x  (as given)
> expand (1+i)^3
  ucas: complex (1+i)^3
(1+i)^3 = -2 + 2i
  Re = -2,  Im = 2
  check: equal numerically (-2 + 2i, complex arithmetic)
